From abe70c0f8650dccbffaa92010c1e9db56d521714 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuda Adi Pratama <yudaadipratama@Yudas-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 17:46:01 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(chatwoot-fork): Cases list with the client's WIP-table
 columns
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

One filterable table over existing Chatwoot conversation data — no new
backend storage or endpoint, per Package D Task 5. Reads
conversation.custom_attributes (division/concern/case_type/channel/dealer,
written by the demo seeder; case_category/case_subcategory as a fallback
for real conversations, per mapping.py) and the contact's custom_attributes
(vehicle_no/purchased_from) embedded at conversation.meta.sender.

Columns: Case ID, Division, Concern, Purchased From, Escalated To, Car
Plate, Aging (days), Status. Filters: division, case type, status, channel,
dealer. Legacy conversations lacking any of these fields render a blank
('—') cell rather than undefined/null/NaN.

- app/javascript/dashboard/api/protonCases.js — data access: paginates
  Chatwoot's own /conversations endpoint and maps each conversation to a
  case row. No useProtonConfig/backend dependency. Caps at MAX_PAGES (1,000
  conversations); fetchAllCases() now returns { rows, truncated,
  fetchedCount, totalCount } so callers can tell when that cap — not the
  ordinary end of the list — is what stopped paging.
- app/javascript/dashboard/components/widgets/ProtonCasesTable.vue — the
  reusable table + filters, kept separate from the page so Package E's
  reporting pages can reuse it against their own case rows.
- app/javascript/dashboard/views/ProtonCasesPage.vue — thin page shell
  (mirrors ProtonRsaPage.vue / ProtonCustomer360Page.vue). Renders the
  core amber Banner component when fetchAllCases() reports truncated, so
  a capped list reads as visibly partial instead of silently hiding cases
  past the 1,000-conversation limit.
- dashboard.routes.js / Sidebar.vue — proton/cases route + nav entry,
  gated on the existing customer360.view permission (there's no backend
  require_permission route to match, since this reads Chatwoot directly).
